Post-Stroke Pain Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Hemibody burning/freezing dysesthesia, Delayed onset post-infarction, Profound thermal allodynia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-046
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of unmanageable, whole-body burning pain and distress preventing any basic self-care or sleep in a stroke survivor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a stroke survivor experiencing an acute, paralyzing crisis of severe, lateralized burning pain and profound thermal allodynia, rendering standard analgesics useless and completely disrupting sleep. Urgent assessment was mandated to rule out an acute recurrent cerebrovascular event, evaluate for silent deep vein thrombosis in the hemiparetic limb, and administer immediate neuropathic pain modulation to arrest acute physiological and psychological decompensation prior to morning services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The entire left side of my body feels like it is plunged into freezing, burning water; even the air moving in the room is excruciating."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a severe acute-on-chronic pain crisis localized entirely to the left hemibody. Timeline: Patient suffered a right-sided thalamic stroke 6 months ago, resulting in residual left hemiparesis. Following a 3-month pain-free period, the patient developed an insidious onset of continuous burning and freezing dysesthesia strictly lateralized to the hemiparetic side. Over the last 48 hours, this pain escalated into a severe crisis, characterized by marked allodynia where light touch from clothing or bedsheets is unbearable. The patient reports severe paroxysmal, spontaneous shooting spasms superimposing upon the continuous baseline pain. An acute drop in the home's ambient temperature this evening profoundly exacerbated the neuropathic pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe pain and allodynia; entirely dependent on the caregiver for transfers and repositioning.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound sleep disruption and severe psychological distress directly related to the unremitting, agonizing nature of the central pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 156/94 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, highly distressed, diaphoretic, actively guarding the entire left side of the body.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals established left-sided hemiparesis (3/5 strength globally on the left) consistent with the known remote stroke, without any new focal motor deficits, facial droop, or acute speech changes. Severe hyperalgesia and thermal allodynia demonstrated on the left hemibody: a mildly cool metal tuning fork produces an exaggerated, prolonged pain response described as "burning ice." Sensation on the right side is entirely normal. Trophic and autonomic changes are noted: the left hand and foot appear slightly mottled and feel distinctly cooler than the right. No unilateral calf swelling or localized erythema to suggest DVT or cellulitis.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s bedroom window was left open, causing a draft that triggered the acute thermal allodynia crisis.
Medication Reconciliation: Took regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen with zero effect on the central neuropathic pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute crisis of Central Post-Stroke Pain Syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Recurrent Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out clinically by the absence of new motor, cranial nerve, or speech deficits compared to baseline, and the purely sensory/pain-driven nature of the acute presentation.

Deep Vein Thrombosis (Left Leg) - Ruled out by the absence of localized calf swelling, negative Homan's sign, and the generalized hemibody distribution of the pain rather than strictly localized limb pain.

Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) - Less likely as the primary diagnosis given the strict hemibody distribution respecting the midline (classic for central thalamic lesions) rather than regional distribution following peripheral limb trauma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of severe distress driven by a massive central pain crisis, a classic delayed complication of a thalamic stroke. The critical clinical task was to firmly differentiate this central sensory "storm" from a new acute ischemic event or a localized DVT in the paretic limb. Clinical examination confirmed the pain was purely neuropathic, mapping precisely to the previous stroke territory, without any new objective neurological deficits. The severity of the allodynia rendered the patient completely immobilized and unable to sleep. Postponing evaluation would have subjected the patient to unmanageable psychological trauma and physical suffering. Urgent after-hours assessment safely excluded life-threatening alternatives, recognized the central neuropathic nature of the crisis, and allowed for the immediate initiation of targeted membrane-stabilizing medications to break the acute sympathetic pain loop.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Pregabalin 75mg STAT. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg BD ongoing. Advised the immediate cessation of ineffective standard NSAIDs/opioids.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a bed cradle or thick cardboard box to physically tent the blankets over the affected side of the body. Consequence: Complete elimination of unpredictable fabric friction against the hypersensitive skin, instantly stopping the sharp, allodynic pain spikes and allowing sleep (negative reinforcement).
Action: Maintain a strict, constant ambient temperature in the bedroom using a space heater, avoiding any cold drafts. Consequence: Prevention of the sudden, agonizing "freezing-burning" pain flares triggered by minor thermal drops in the environment (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized Pain Medicine physician via the primary GP.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to titrate neuropathic medications and arrange specialist pain clinic follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of any new physical weakness on either side, slurred speech, facial drooping, or sudden confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, representing permanent damage to the brain's pain processing center. Standard painkillers are completely ineffective for this condition. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Central post-stroke pain is notoriously difficult to treat. The new nerve medication will take several days to build up in the system and begin "turning down the volume" of the pain signals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you notice your speech slurring, if your face droops, or if you develop new weakness on your "good" side.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The stroke damaged the part of your brain that processes feeling. It is now misfiring and translating normal sensations, like a cool breeze or a blanket, into severe burning pain. Standard painkillers do not work because the tissue is not damaged; the brain itself is sending a false alarm. The new medication works specifically to calm down those misfiring brain cells.
Actionable Steps: Tent the blankets tonight so nothing touches the skin. Keep the room temperature completely stable. You must give the new medication several days to work; do not stop taking it just because the pain does not vanish instantly.

